# Auto-build after build fix series (Ledgerly ERP)

When you finish a **connected set of changes** that fix or materially affect **compilation, packaging, installers, CI, auth/startup, or runnable client/server behavior**, do **not** end the turn until you have:

1. Build Release:
   ```powershell
   dotnet build src/Ledgerly.Shared -c Release
   dotnet build src/Ledgerly.Server -c Release
   dotnet build src/Ledgerly.Client -c Release
   ```
2. If desktop/server behavior changed, stop old processes and smoke-launch:
   ```powershell
   Get-Process Ledgerly.Client,Ledgerly.Server -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Stop-Process -Force
   Start-Process src\Ledgerly.Server\bin\Release\net48\Ledgerly.Server.exe -WorkingDirectory src\Ledgerly.Server
   Start-Sleep -Seconds 2
   Start-Process src\Ledgerly.Client\bin\Release\net48-windows\Ledgerly.Client.exe -WorkingDirectory src\Ledgerly.Client
   ```
3. In the reply, give **full paths** to the `.exe` files to run.
4. Paste a **### New build links** block (see `release-handoff-and-readme-sop.mdc`) with **Combined + Client + Server** download URLs from `gh release view` — **never invent** URLs. If only local binaries exist, give paths under `installers\` for all three `*Setup.exe` files.

Do this **automatically**; do not wait for “build it,” “so we can test,” or “give me download links.”

When packaging installers (shipping drop / release SOP), **always** run `build_installers.ps1` so Combined, Client-only, and Server-only are all produced and linked.

Also **always** complete the full ship pipeline in `release-handoff-and-readme-sop.mdc` (commit, push, GitHub Release with all three installers, README Downloads, profile downloads if available). Do not ask permission to release after a shipping-ready fix series.
